Hi all, I'm a LAN administrator here at the University of Alabama and
I have recently been informed that the message below should be placed 
on the logon screens of all mainframe and LAN systems on campus.

**********************************************************************
Use of this system is limited to authorized personnel for University 
purposes only. All activity on this system is subject to inspection 
to ensure compliance with this policy.
**********************************************************************

I am against the use of this so called "security banner" for a number 
of reasons: (1) the language of the statement is vague, (2) it seems
to validate any snooping system administrators might choose to do,
(3) it does little, if anything, to improve security, and (4) it leads
users to believe that big brother is watching.  According to one of
the instigators of this policy, an institution must have a banner
like this on its logon screens to be able to prosecute "hackers"
who break into the system.  It's my belief that no statement about
authorized access is needed.  After all, we don't put signs on our
front doors stating that our homes are for authorized use only.  If
someone breaks into my home, he/she has clearly commited a crime,
warning or no warning.  Wouldn't the same thing be true for computer
systems?  

So what do you guys think?  Is this sort of banner needed?  Is it
legal for a public University to carry out this sort of "inspection?" 
What laws/principles are applicable here?  Any opinions welcome.
