CERT, an organization changed with protecting user privacy, is likely
the cause of one of the largest threats to user privacy on the Net, a
threat to the fourth amendment rights of every authorized computer
user at a state university.

According to attorney Mike Riddle, it all started when the Department
of Justice thought that the ECPA had a small ambiguity or "bug" in it
that might allow an unauthorized intruder get some evidence thrown out
court. CERT took the Justice department's general warning to federal
computer systems and turned it into a specific sample banner for
(apparently) all computer systems. The CAF archive contains 10
university's versions of CERT's suggested banner.

These university banners are a travesty. Instead of fixing the little
bug in the ECPA, they strip all university computers users of
virtually all privacy protection. I'm enclosing a file from the CAF
archive with more details.

CERT should withdrawal its suggested banner. CERT was created to give
good technical advice, not to give bad legal advice. No university (or
any other organization) site should install a "CERT" banner without
(at least) talking to their university legal advisor and the
university policy makers.

- Carl

