I don't know how McGill manages to do it, but they always seem to rub
people the wrong way in terms of privacy. Here is the legal opinion of
Raynald Mercille who is the legal advisor of McGill University 
concerning our rights to personal privacy.
=======================================================================	
	McGill				MEMORANDUM	25 January 1994

FROM:	Dr. S.J.Noumoff
	Member of Senate

TO:	Mr. Raynald Mercille
	Legal Advisor

DATE:	January 25, 1994

SUBJECT:	Question

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Does the University believe that it has the right to access all material
housed within University property, be it office or lab, save sealed mail
and/or a closed briefcase or like container?

If possible I'd be most grateful for an indication of your response,
in order to table a properly phrased question for Senate itself.
I can be reached either at XXX or XXX.

Many thanks.

(signed)

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

	McGill				MEMORANDUM


FROM:	Raynald Mercille
	Legal Advisor

TO:	Professor S.J.Noumoff
	Member of Senate

CC:	Vice-Principal F.Tavenas
	Ms. Victoria Lees,
	Secretary of Senate

DATE:	February 1, 1994

SUBJECT:	University's right to access

---------------------------------------------------------------------------

I write in reply to your of January 25, 1994.

Generally, the University does have a right to access all material
housed within University property, not only to office space or lab
but also, mail, closed briefcases or similar containers.  This is
a right enjoyed by all owners of buildings or property generally
and it is usually tied to any number of obligations and statutory
duties.  For example, if the University was accused of illegal
pirating of software under the Criminal Code, it could not argue
legally that it cannot access its own premises or equipment.  Such
a defense would be summarily dismissed by a court as an abdication
of the University's responsibility before the law.

In fact in some cases, the University would have the right and duty
to revendicate University property kept at home by an academic
staff, to abide by some statutory duties.  For example, the Access
Law provides for certain right of access by persons concerned that
documents respecting them if such documents are held by public
bodies.  To illustrate the point, personal files about student X
held in a professor's home are documents held by the University
under the Access Law.  Student X could not lose a right to see the
documents simply because they are held at the home of an employee.

There is however, a test of a need to know by the University
administrator, so that in each case access is not merely a question
of pure arbitrariness.  In addition, each right to access must be
seen within its procedural context.

In the above example of documents held at home, the University's
right of access if denied by the professor is exercised by a
bailiff using a writ issued by the court.  In the case of the mail,
the right of access would be subject to the strictures of the
Postal Act.  With respect to access to office space, an authorized
representative of the university, which may include maintenance and
security staff, from the janitor to senior administrators, could on
a need to know basis, without specific permission, enter into the
academic's office space and inspect and remove all its contents,
furnishings and structure.  If personal property, such as paintings
or papers or other belongings happen to be present, they of course
are segregated and held for the account of the academic.

If the nature of a certain duty under the law (ie. respect for
computer software copyright) justifies an on-going procedure of
entry without specific notice or consent, the University can apply
such a procedure.  Software auditing is simply the mandatory
procedure required to permit the University to determine that it is
not in breach of copyright law to protect its staff and itself.

It is undisputed that in the event of fire or bomb alert, the
University has the unquestioned right to enter into an academic's
office and plow through all its contents, regardless of ownership.
I could go on forever with examples.  This illustrates that the
perception of privilege of privacy is wrong in law, although the
University's traditional reserve goes a long way to supporting such
a state of affairs.

