The words "right" and "privilege" have no clear, distinct legal
meaning. For example, the U.S. Constitution refers to "privilege of
the writ of habeas corpus". But many would call "habeas corpus" a
right.  A credit card company says "membership has its privileges" and
yet many of these "privileges" are spelled out by contract and are
thus could also be called contractual rights.

I'm going to avoid using either word. Here are my claims:

Given due process a student can be denied access to university services.
Students should not be denied access to univeristy services arbitrarily.

Would anyone else care to skip over discussion of definitions and join
me in discussing the meat of the matter?

