>has published an article disclosing the circumstances surrounding the deaths
>of the victims of Karla Homolka and Paul Teale.
> 
>"That Homolka Thing..." appears in the February 28 issue.

Our anonymous friend makes this article sound like a big deal.  However, a
simple reading of the article shows that it is an empassioned plea by
a former resident of the Southern Ontario area in which she explains
what it was like to live there at that time.  She mentions a number
of established and publishable facts including the statement of
Karla Homolka's conviction and the reaction of the press in attendance
at the trial (visibly shaken, driven to tears, etc.)  The author
of the article asks why anyone would want to know the details.

You can choose to argue her point on the grounds of the right to know
or the freedom of information, but I have no interest in diving into
that well-worn debate.

However, it is a disservice to pronounce this article as
being "in open defiance of the [...] publication ban".  In my opinion,
and based on facts published legally elsewhere within Canada, and
