Here is what I think is wrong:
   1) If there is no reasonable danger to people, property etc., it
      is an unfair punishment before "conviction".
   2) It removes the authorities' incentive to act promptly.
   3) It cuts the accused off Net resources that might help him
      prepare his case and get a fair hearing.
   4) It treats Net access differently than other university resources
      and gives sys admin more authority than professors.
      (In 1904 professors at my university, the U. of Illinois, could
       on their own suspend students from class for up to 3 days.
       Thankfully, professors here no longer have such unchecked power.)

