[Carl Kadie says I don't have title to alt.homosexual, and should create it
 moderated if I want it.]

Let me back up a bit.

- I am in favor of free speech.  Even when forwarding the occasional message
to a sysadmin when I felt a posting was inappropriate, I have specified
that I did not wish to interfere with the poster's right to free speech,
but still felt the post was inappropriate and wished to do something more
about it.

- In each case where I had done so I had previously contacted the person
directly via e-mail first explaining why I thought their postings seemed,
to me, inappropriate.  Chuck responded by telling me I was "a looser, all
that was wrong with America, a goddamned fucking queer", for example.  He
then proceeded to call me "Larry the Queer" in public postings.  Granted,
I had little fear for life or limb, but all the same this is not the 
mature, rational behavior we expect of other adults.

- In Chuck's case, I felt repeated verbal harassment from him was getting
tiring.  If someone calls you "faggot" in a shopping mall for a while you
ignore it, hoping they'll go away.  If it keeps up you go pester a security
guard to keep them from following you about the mall.  Having tired of
Chuck, I pestered the local security guard (his sysadmin).

Carl, you are right when you say that I don't hold title on alt.homosexual.
I have never claimed to.  However, I do claim that there is a degree of
human discretion called for when posting to any group.  For example, try
posting a personal ad to alt.sex.bondage.  Or posting a long flame about
how Jews are cheap or whatever into soc.culture.jewish.  Or a long rant
about how believing in G*d shows that all Christians are infantile,
Oedipal fanatics to soc.religion.christian.

In any of the above cases, a single post or two will probably be ignored,
or start a minor flamewar.  The discussion may or may not get interesting.
But once having started the discussion if you were to start flooding
any of the newsgroups with "You're a fucking Kike" [Ed. I've always wondered,
does anyone know what the derivation of this one is???], or "You're
a bunch of AIDS-spreading sickos", or "Hey, Larry the Queer, you're
a fucking asshole - you're all that's wrong with America", well, you
can turn the other cheek for a while, but if it keeps up eventually you
get tired of it.

When it reaches a fever pitch, you might decide (as I did), that the
poster is out of control, and someone should go gently knock them on
the noggin and tell them that although only candy-assed faggots like
Larry the Queer don't like name-calling and while that is not a reason
to restrict free speech that all the same responsible adults could
behave on the net with a bit more decorum.

Essentially, I don't like being called faggot to my face, and I don't like
it electronically.  I already spend far too much time for my own good
"just chilling" and letting things roll off me.  I asked via private
e-mail for it to stop, and when that didn't work I escalated it to the
only other person I could reach - the sysadmin.  

I think that part of defending Free Speech is helping to create an
environment where people can feel they can express their views without
being threatened.  I think part of making Free Speech something more
than we give lip service to is to make reasoned, responsible Free
Speech a part of our repertoire.

We must consider the effect of our Free Speech on others.  This is not
to say that we cannot utter what is on our minds, even if it might be
considered by some as hateful.  It is important, though, to remember that
there are consequences to any action we make, spoken or otherwise.  We
may steal if we are willing to pay the possible consequences, moral
and legal.  Likewise we may send hate-mail to people if we are prepared
to face the possible consequences.

					larry...




