>the additional burden.

Readers of CAF-Talk have been parachuted into the middle of this bouhaha, so
this is admittedly written without knowing the whole story.  It seems to me,
solely on the basis of the post which contained the above, that this user may
indeed have committed abuses, which may have made the admin less tolerant.
Still, it's disturbing that the latter deems the speech of a "guest" user
inherently less deserving of protection than that of users with a current
affiliation, other things being equal.  By the admin's own admission, the
complaints about the user's posts were often unfounded.  Yet he seems
inclined to defend the user in question, less readily then others, simply
on the basis of the hassle factor.  I detect an uncomfortable analogy to
the McCarthy-era blacklists in the entertainment industry, in which performers
against whom there was no sound evidence of subversion were let go anyway,
because of the nuisance their bosses perceived from the radical right.  It also
seems to me that a constitutional equal protection issue may exist, if some
users are avowedly being denied the same level of free speech afforded to
others on the basis of their particular relationship to the university.

