In article <CMErFy.44s@cs.uiuc.edu>, kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes...
>rachellm@unvax.union.edu (Morgan Rachell) writes:
> 
>[...]
>>I was in a situation where my account was searched without my knowledge.
>>The sysadmin "had reason to believe my account was being used for nonacademic
>>purposes" (college computer, of course).  I asked him as to the legality of
>>searching my account without authorization and he said that the computer
>>is property of the school.  Therefore, (essentially) the school and/or the
>>sysadmins can do whatever they want with any account on the system.
>[...]
> 
>While such searches may be legal at private universities (it depends
>on the Student Code), I believe they are highly unethical. 

I only wish that mattered in the real world... At least in the U.S., the
term "Justice System" is becoming increasingly oxymoronic. (In some cases
-- not necessarily involving computers in any way -- the emphasis should
be on "moronic"!)  I agree fully with your feelings on the ethics of such
an action... but legally, there are very few places it would give either
of us a leg to stand on (again, that's here in the USA, I don't know the
laws outside our borders).

I'd have to spend some time to find the articles & documents with the
details (time I don't necessarily have), but there exists in the U.S. a
solid precedent that states the entity that owns a system -- be it a
university, a corporation, or a small "mom & pop" enterprise -- has the
right to do all kinds of nasty things, including (but not limited to)
"eavesdropping" on keystrokes and/or screen images; reviewing Email;
limiting use according to proprietary criteria (so long as they're
documented); and searching through the contents of user directories.

I do _not_ like this, but that's how the law here works.  :-(

>Also, remember even though your university does own the computers, the
>sys admin doesn't.

Yes, but that SysAdmin is a legal representative of the computer owners,
and is thus empowered to do whatever the owner has said is alright; this
often (not always, but often) includes acting on his/her own intuition
to make sure that nothing "distasteful" to the owner is done to/with/on
the system.

>Does your university really trust a sys admin to,
>on his or her own, search the computer files of professors and
>supervisors? Does it trust him or her to understand and comply with
>the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act and the Electronic
>Commincations Privacy Act?  For state universities, can he or he be
>trusted to understand the 5th Amendment rights of students and staff?

I've known SysAdmins who can't even spell "5th," much less understand
what document was amended... :-(  Luckily, I've also known a few who
I would have been happy to have as the drafters of any laws they cared
to write; it's all the luck of the draw.  Of course, if a SysAdmin at
a site does something spiteful simply because s/he doesn't like some
user _but_ is a good enough talker and the next level(s) of management
aren't really tied in to what's going on, that SysAdmin can get away
with murder (figuratively speaking, I hope!).

>Here at U. of Illinois there was enough outrage when a department
>claimed authority to conduct arbitrary searches that the University
>now explicitly forbids arbitrary searches.

Just out of curiosity... does that include digital/electronic searches
(or searches of such a domain), or just searches of a *physical* site?

-- Brian

