In article <2ljqho$fgq@Mercury.mcs.com> karl@MCS.COM (Karl Denninger) writes:
...
> Yes, this is a US-centric discussion and point of view.  All the parties
> involved here are US residents posting from US sites.  Those sites outside
> of the US which have more restrictive policies or laws are the ones which
> are responsible for any required filtering -- not the originator which
> falls under US law (in this case)

There is a wider issue here I think, and one that has not been yet
addressed by the legal system.

First, let me say that I will have *NO* truck with prior constraint.
I consider that there is NEVER a case to prevent a person speaking.
Having spoken though, he is then responsible for the result.

The news groups are a form of community, bound, not geographically, as
is custom, but by common interest, purpose and mutual consent. If an
individual violates the mores and cannons of any community, they
are invited to desist, or leave.

If there is not some measure of common sense used, the news group can
be trashed by a few 'poisoning' the group. The reaction is to then
form a moderated group, with the inevitable chilling effect.

So we need to consider carefully;  do we wish to be able to defend
our space and freedom to exchange views in a cordial, communal way;
or do we have a more anarchic freedom?

BTW, read carefully before you quote...

