In article <2lq4v5$jo@mail.fwi.uva.nl>,
Casper H.S. Dik <casper@fwi.uva.nl> wrote:

> Becoming a legal adult does not change you.  It does not ``magically''
> makes you behave more responsibly.

Sad enough, that's true. But legal adults are *expected* to behave
responsibly in other areas too. Any 18-year-old here can put himself in
debt he could never pay off for the rest of his life, and the
[German] Federal Court holds that this is entirely his own business.
(While we're at the subject of ruining one's life. We have no
bankruptcy law for a single person's property in Germany (yet).)

It is interesting to compare this with *criminal* law (in Germany,
again). The court can decide whether to punish a convict by the rules
for minors until they are 21, but it can also decide to apply the
rules for adults (meaning much higher maximum jail terms, among other
conditions) for an 18-year old. AFAIK, this is the only case where the
law acknowledges the different individual degrees of maturity (and not
even that, strictly speaking, it's rather a reminiscence of the times
when the legal adulthood was at 21).

But I doubt you'll find any judge who would agree that it takes you to
be at least 25 years for telling right from wrong in such a simple
case as intruding others' property (as system cracking is). Where
we're not allowed to trespass, we've learned when still in
pre-elementary school age.

> I'd rather not tempt the students around here by publishing all the
> scripts etc.  Most of them probably don't have the time or aren't even
> interested in running those scripts, some may be interested but know
> that its wrong.  But the few that might be tempted, we'd rather keep
> in check.  Less hassle for us, less hassle for them.  If you call that

Unfortunately, at least when it comes to computers, this attitude is
prevalent in many educational institutions. "Protect the little
children from themselves." I wonder why. When entering the university
library, even an 18-year-old freshman is expected to behave
accordingly - there you don't usually put up fences at the stairs and
all the other devices for securing little children...

Or, from a slightly different point of view: You're not supposed to
steal books from the library, and you're supposed to recognize the
reasons for this. You're not supposed to break into your university's
computers, and that's exactly the same thing.

> `paternalistic'', so be it.

But how else would you call that?

> But don't give me the ``old enough to die for their country''.
> 18 year olds are precisely the right age to die for their country,
> precisely because they are old enough physically to fight, and
> young enough mentally to be molded in to soldiers.

That's also true, unfortunately. But this doesn't imply that they're
necessarily too young to make conscious decisions about right and
wrong in such complicated cases as whether to refuse military service,
else not so many of them would do so [again a German special, but
perhaps Americans reading this will remember the Vietnam War.] Even
more in the trivial case of deciding whether to resist the
"temptation" to run a script that would give you extra privileges on a
machine by exploiting an OS bug. There is no barrier between the world
of computing and the "real" world.

