A similar issue was the subject to the mock court session at CFP '93. 
There the question concerned the liability of a sysop for allowing computer
viruses on his system.  Without doing any research on the question, my gut
reaction is that you should not make any statement about your knowledge of
the pirating activities (assuming it is knowledge rather than mere
suspicion) just as a matter of principle, although technically it would be
hearsay -- though if you are a sysop it may come under the business records
exception if your system records all e-mail transmissions.  As to whether
you could be held liable as an accomplice to the pirating activities, the
chances are much better if there is evidence that you were aware of such
activities and failed to do anything about them.  On the other side of the
coin is the user's right to privacy, which given the current judicial
climate, is questionable, especially if the judge is ignorant regarding
computers, which most are.

