There are really two questions. Is it censorship? and if it is,
is such censorship wrong at a Catholic university?

I won't try to answer the second question, but I recommend _Academic
freedom and Catholic higher education_ by James John Annarelli (1987)
and "The School Library Media Specialist: Dealing with Complaints
about Materials" by Dianne McAff Hopkins in _Catholic Library World_,
Vol 56, No. 4, pp. 172-174, November 1984.

As to the first question, I would say that if secular library and
secular university are applied, it would censorship because it is
restriction of material based on "partisan or doctrinal disapproval."
[From the Library Bill of Rights.]

>Keep in mind that the people here have access to 
>IRC and can also get an account on the Cleveland Freenet (which has many more
>of the NEWS groups than we get to start with).  also, should not these people
>be happy that they get any news at all, as some schools have much less access
>to internet than we do.

By secular library standards, the effectiveness of a ban or
restriction is irrelevant, what matters is the motivation. 

- Carl

