Who wants to to be first to cancel their Denver Post subscription?

I saw a street vendor selling a Sunday Post Bulldog edition (i.e., published
on Saturday) and thought I'd see what it had to say...

Well, first there's a story on page 4A about some cop in PA who tracks
pedophiles on BBSs...  but that's just to whet our appeties:

Page 1B, "Local techie rides posse on computer network" by Kevin Simpson.

I'm pretty enraged right now.  I am attributed as saying things when the
reporter NEVER TALKED TO ME, and, in fact, never gave me a sporting chance
to talk to him (oh, yeah, he gives me a couple hour window to receive my
voice mail and call him back, when I happened to be otherwise occupied, and
didn't get the voice mail until after he'd left and written the article).

Yes, he read some Hester-provided postings of mine, and makes it sound like I
talked to him, but the whole article naturally has a Tim Hester, Good Guy slant
to it. Surpising, considering the reporter never talked with anyone else
to verify his facts.  [Hey, did I predict this or what?]

Here's the paragraphs about Nyx (which come toward the very end, after running
on and on about how evil the group is):

	...
	Hester's dim view of pedophilia, while certainly shared by
	the vast majority of on-line users, nonetheless runs head-on
	into the delicate issue of censorship.  Specifically, he would
	like Nyx, which is the University of Denver-based entrance ramp
	to the info superhighway network, to delete the alt.sex.intergen
	newsgroup.  DU, of course, had nothing to do with its creation and,
	in fact, issues a message noting that the university "disclaims
	everything it can relating Nyx" as soon as users log on.

	Nevertheless, Hester contends that local system administrator
	Andrew Burt, a DU computer science professor, has the authority
	to delete the gruop from those that can be accessed through Nyx.
	But explains in a network posting that Nyx regards its role as
	similar to the phone company, which does not attempt to monitor,
	much less censor, all the information that passes along its lines.

	Understandably, Burt doesn't want the job of unilateral censor.
	And he isn't about to wade into the moral and ethical morass that
	would result from passing judgment on alt.sex.intergen -- or any
	other group on the network.

	You can't blame him for that.  And you can't blame Hester, the
	father of two school-age daughters, for bristling at the appearance
	of a nationwide computer niche unabashedly tailored to adults who
	condone sex with children
	...

So, I come out sounding like a supporter of pedophiles, or at least like
someone who doesn't take the issue seriously; the article sort of cries out
for people to call DU and demand they, ah, "help" me make the decision to
censor the group.  (What? He has the power to stop this and he doesn't?!?)
