Abstract of CAF-News
[Week ending mar 13 1994

========================== KEY ================================
The words after the numbers are a short PARAPHRASES of the
articles, or QUOTES from them, NOT AN OBJECTIVE SUMMARY and
not necessarily my opinion.
===============================================================

[nyx.misc, et al.]  Pedofiles and Usenet
    [paraphrase: The sys admin at Nyx, a free public access computer
    service affiliated with the U. of Denver, seems nervous that a user's
    complaints about carring newsgroup alt.sex.intergen might mean an end
    of University support. Nothing in alt.sex.intergen is illegal, the
    complainer just "does not like what the subscribers to [the newsgroup]
    have to say."]
    <2lteuo$3av@eff.org>

Imminent Death of Nyx Predicted
    [paraphrase: [A computer science professor and creator of NYX, a free
    public access service affiliated with Denver University:] The Denver
    Post ran story today [Sunday] about our carrying alt.sex.intergen.
    They didn't even talk to me. They didn't mention that the newsgroup
    doesn't seem to actually have blatantly obscene material in it, just
    discussions of "is pedophilia ok/evil", etc.). "I'm not entirely sure
    what to suggest; the nail may already be in Nyx's coffin when the
    [University administrators] read [the article]."
    <1994Mar13.023512.20908@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu>

IS IT CENSORSHIP OR NO?
    [parapharse: A sys admin at the U. of Scranton, a Catholic U. in
    Pennsylvania, has removed newsgroups he found offensive because he
    felt the newsgroups were 'ramming their opinions down people's
    throughts [thoughts?]'  and were entirely un-Christian.]
    <1994Mar12.192246.1@jaguar.uofs.edu>

Right to access?
    [paraphrase: [McGill Daily] The University claims authority to search
    anything and anyone on campus. Unlike the police who must get higher
    authorization *before* a search, the University claims that its agents
    need only be able to justify a search, if asked, *after* the search.
    The University's claim seem to violate common sense and the laws of
    Quebec.]
    <1994Mar8.060846.5949@sifon.cc.mcgill.ca>

Detweiler censored
    [paraphrase: [A sys admin at Colorado State U.:] I terminated a former
    student's curtsey account because responding to daily "questionable
    and usually frivolous" complaints about his posts was taking too much
    of my time. "Had Mr.  Detweiler been a faculty member, enrolled
    student, or had any other significant relationship with the
    University, I might have been more inclined to champion his cause."]
    <1994Mar9.150723.114504@yuma>

Harassment
    [paraphrase: [A student at the State University of New York Institute
    for Technology (SUNY-IT):] My sys admin forced me, against my wishes,
    to allow someone I don't like into the Internet Relay Chat (IRC)
    channel I operate.]
    <1994Mar8.205430.10047@pool.info.sunyit.edu>

RE: Sysadmins searching through accounts? (RFC)
    [paraphrase: The U. of Winnipeg in Canada requires users to sign away
    any privacy rights they might have as a condition of getting a university
    computer account.]
    <11MAR94.16524702@uwpg02.uwinnipeg.ca>

Sysadmins searching through accounts? (RFC)
    [paraphrase: "the sysadmin at a college unit site [of the University
    of Massachusetts - Lowell]" ... "has been known to: read almost ALL
    mail between student" [and ...] "suspend accounts at his whim"]
    <RiT2ic5w165w@works.com>



