   Markku.Savela@tel.vtt.fi (VTT/TEL) writes:
   >In article <1994Mar9.140832.24616@news.cs.indiana.edu> "Congratulations...you have just discovered the secret message." <sconway@mango.ucs.indiana.edu> writes:
   >>Really?  Yuck!  Sounds to me as if you need to go over your lamer sysop's
   >>head and complain...LOUDLY!  Dave is right IRC == toy, not real life, and

   >It maybe be a toy for you and Dave, but don't generalize your personal
   >opinion to others. Not everyone uses IRC as "toy".

   >If these "toy users" or "gamesters" prevent the non-toy use of IRC,
   >sysadmins better get involved.

   Hoo boy!  Here we go again.  Sounds like you must be in cahoots with that
   aforementioned incredible lamer sysop in the earlier post.
   Well, since I bet you are (*laugh*), I have a message you may want to give
   him:  Get a life and leave people alone so you can go back to playing
   Solitaire in Microsoft Windows.

You really don't seem to get the point of what Markku Savela is
saying. Some people are actually trying to make use of IRC to do real
academic and research work. I sometimes wonder why they bother when
there's a whole bucketload of selfish people out there who are quite
content to cause disruption on a wide scale and not give a toss about
the consequences - in fact, I think they sometimes wonder too. He
isn't saying that people shouldn't use it for anything they like -
just that they should be considerate network users. If a user isn't
being cooperative, their administrators -should- get involved and
educate them. If they are the administrators, then they're the ones
who have got a *real* problem with their life.

The people trying to do real work are often the ones who are
developing stuff- they'll just stop supporting it. If the people who
run the computer systems decide that IRC can't be used for anything
useful, they too will stop supporting it (at the moment, many places
do support it, which is why it continues - and this is -good-).

Like Markku, I don't care what people use IRC for, as long as they
don't disrupt and affect the service for -anyone- else. (Do you -really-
think that the ability of the endless streams of warbots are a good
thing, and don't seriously affect the service for everyone?)

James.
